---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { COUNTS, REQUIREMENTS, spell } from '../../data/docs';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../data/site';

const entry = page('/docs/getting-started/');

const build = `git clone https://github.com/thatcube/hozz.git
cd hozz

# Signing lives in a gitignored Local.xcconfig:
echo "DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = YOURTEAMID" > Local.xcconfig

# Build and install on a connected iPhone:
tools/device-build.sh`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    Hozz has two halves that can be used independently. The <strong>iPhone app</strong> reads Apple
    Health and sends it somewhere. The <strong>Mac app</strong> is one of the places it can send it
    to, and adds charts and assistant access. You only need the Mac app if you want it.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install it</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz is early alpha and <strong>not in the App Store yet</strong>, so today installing it means
    building it. That needs a Mac with Xcode {REQUIREMENTS.xcode} or newer and{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/yonaskolb/XcodeGen">XcodeGen</a> {REQUIREMENTS.xcodegen} or newer.
  </p>

  <Code caption="Terminal" code={build} />

  <p>
    <code>tools/mac-build.sh</code> does the same for the Mac app.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="One thing that will bite you on a device build">
    <p>
      The app and widget targets declare the App Group <code>group.com.thatcube.Hozz</code>. Your
      Apple developer profile has to carry that group or a device build with the widget fails. This
      does not affect Simulator builds.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>2. Give it Health access</h2>

  <p>
    On first run Hozz asks Health for the types it reads. Grant what you are comfortable with — you
    can grant a subset, and you can change it later in{' '}
    <strong>Settings → Health → Data Access &amp; Devices → Hozz</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>There is one consequence of Apple's design worth knowing before you see it:</p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Hozz cannot tell “denied” from “you have none”">
    <p>
      HealthKit deliberately refuses to reveal which types you withheld, because telling an app
      would leak the fact that you have data you chose not to share. So a type you denied and a type
      you genuinely have no records of look identical to Hozz.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rather than guess, Hozz reports the state it actually has and says so in those words. If a
      type shows no data and you expected some, check the Health settings screen above.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>3. Pick a destination</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing leaves your phone until you add a destination and confirm it. There is no default
    destination, because a default would be a place someone else chose.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the <strong>Automatic</strong> tab, add a destination. Hozz lists any Macs it can find on
    your network first, then {spell(COUNTS.presets)} presets: Folder, Home Assistant, InfluxDB, Web address, and MQTT.{' '}
    <a href="/docs/destinations/">Choosing a destination</a> covers what each is for; if you have no
    strong preference, <a href="/docs/destinations/folder/">Folder</a> is the one Hozz recommends,
    because it needs no server, no open ports and no VPN.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each destination has its own settings: which Health types it receives, which format, and how
    often. Use <strong>Send a test</strong> before you trust it. A test sends a small probe in the
    destination's own format and reports what came back — it contains no Health data.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Run the first export</h2>

  <h3>Automatic sync</h3>

  <p>
    The first automatic sync works through your history a bounded batch at a time. It gives every
    selected type a share of each pass rather than draining one type to exhaustion before starting
    the next — a phone with years of stand hours would otherwise send nothing else for days, which
    is a working sync that reads exactly like a broken one.
  </p>

  <p>
    The phone reports how many selected types are complete; the Mac reports how many types have
    arrived and how far back they reach. Neither shows a percentage or a time estimate, and that is
    deliberate: Health will not say how many records a type holds without reading all of them, so
    any fraction would be invented.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="A first backfill looks slow because it is">
    <p>
      Years of Health history is a lot of records, and iOS grants background time in short windows.
      A first sync taking days is normal, not a fault. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">
        Troubleshooting</a
      > covers how to tell a slow backfill from a stuck one.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3>Manual export</h3>

  <p>
    On the <strong>Export</strong> tab, Hozz builds a full historical export you can save or share
    from the phone, in NDJSON, CSV, JSON, SQLite, Markdown or GPX. It is resumable: pausing,
    backgrounding, an expiry, a kill or a reboot all resume from the last durable checkpoint rather
    than starting over.
  </p>

  <p>
    Manual export does not depend on a destination and does not disturb one.
  </p>

  <h2>What to read next</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/background-sync/">Background sync</a> — why an export scheduled for 8am does
      not run at 8am, and what Hozz reports instead of pretending it did.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/formats/">Export formats</a> — which of the {spell(COUNTS.exportFormats)} keeps
      everything, and which {spell(COUNTS.lossyExportFormats)} deliberately do not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/data-coverage/">Data coverage</a> — what Hozz exports today and what it does
      not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={GITHUB_URL}>The source</a> — every claim on these pages is meant to be checkable
      there.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
